WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.635
This lesson builds the derivative from first
principles.

2
00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:08.987
We will begin with an average rate of change
over an interval, shrink that interval using a

3
00:00:08.987 --> 00:00:11.505
limit, and obtain the derivative at one point.

4
00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:17.322
Then we will turn that pointwise idea into a
derivative function, recognize where

5
00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:22.691
differentiability fails, interpret motion, and
use a tangent line for local approximation.

6
00:00:22.691 --> 00:00:26.364
The goal is not merely to memorize f prime
notation.

7
00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:30.818
It is to understand what information a
derivative contains, how the definition

8
00:00:30.818 --> 00:00:33.788
produces it, and when that information is
valid.

9
00:00:33.788 --> 00:00:40.020
Keep one question in view: how can we describe
change at an exact input when an ordinary

10
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.219
slope calculation needs two different points?

11
00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:46.100
Choose an input a and a second input a plus h.

12
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.294
The change in input is h.

13
00:00:48.294 --> 00:00:55.574
The corresponding change in output is f of a
plus h minus f of a. Dividing output change by

14
00:00:55.574 --> 00:00:57.873
input change gives the difference quotient.

15
00:00:57.873 --> 00:01:02.567
Geometrically, it is the slope of the secant
line through the two points.

16
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:06.342
In an application, it is an average rate
across the interval.

17
00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:12.216
If position changes by one hundred meters over
five seconds, the average velocity is twenty

18
00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:18.090
meters per second, even if the object moved
faster or slower within those five seconds.

19
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.832
Average change summarizes an interval.

20
00:01:20.832 --> 00:01:24.595
It does not yet isolate what happens at a
single instant.

21
00:01:24.722 --> 00:01:28.498
To focus on the input a, let h approach zero.

22
00:01:28.498 --> 00:01:34.365
We do not substitute h equals zero into the
quotient; that would divide by zero.

23
00:01:34.365 --> 00:01:40.182
Instead, we examine values of h that are
nonzero but arbitrarily small.

24
00:01:40.182 --> 00:01:46.712
As the second point approaches the first, the
secant lines may approach a limiting line.

25
00:01:46.712 --> 00:01:53.315
If their slopes approach one finite value from
both sides, that value is f prime of a, the

26
00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:54.416
derivative at a.

27
00:01:54.416 --> 00:01:57.465
The limit is the crucial reasoning step.

28
00:01:57.465 --> 00:02:03.115
It converts a family of nearby average rates
into one instantaneous rate.

29
00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:09.375
If the slopes do not settle to a common finite
value, the derivative at that point does not

30
00:02:09.375 --> 00:02:09.723
exist.

31
00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:12.609
Let f of x equal x squared.

32
00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:20.015
Start with f of a plus h minus f of a, all
divided by h. Expanding gives a squared plus

33
00:02:20.015 --> 00:02:24.458
two a h plus h squared minus a squared,
divided by h.

34
00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:26.614
The a-squared terms cancel.

35
00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:32.008
Because h is nonzero during the limiting
process, factor h from the numerator and

36
00:02:32.008 --> 00:02:33.935
cancel it with the denominator.

37
00:02:33.935 --> 00:02:36.270
The quotient becomes two a plus h.

38
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.361
Now let h approach zero.

39
00:02:38.361 --> 00:02:45.458
The limit is two a. Notice the order of
operations: form the quotient, simplify it for

40
00:02:45.458 --> 00:02:48.119
nonzero h, then evaluate the limit.

41
00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:53.043
We never claim that h itself is zero while it
remains in the denominator.

42
00:02:53.186 --> 00:02:58.429
The previous calculation kept a symbolic, so
it did more than find one slope.

43
00:02:58.429 --> 00:03:04.896
It produced a rule: for f of x equals x
squared, f prime of x equals two x.

44
00:03:04.896 --> 00:03:11.133
This new function reports the original curve's
tangent slope at every differentiable input.

45
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.730
At x equals three, the derivative is six.

46
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.518
At x equals negative two, it is negative four.

47
00:03:18.518 --> 00:03:20.776
At zero, it is zero.

48
00:03:20.776 --> 00:03:24.296
The derivative function has its own graph and
domain.

49
00:03:24.296 --> 00:03:29.375
Its input is still an x-value from the
original problem, while its output is a rate

50
00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:30.010
or slope.

51
00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:36.734
Thinking of f prime as a function is essential
for later topics such as optimization and

52
00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:37.574
differential equations.

53
00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.200
The sign of the derivative summarizes local
direction.

54
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:48.187
Where f prime is positive, tangent lines rise
from left to right, so the function is locally

55
00:03:48.187 --> 00:03:48.598
increasing.

56
00:03:48.598 --> 00:03:54.861
Where f prime is negative, tangent lines fall,
so the function is locally decreasing.

57
00:03:54.861 --> 00:03:59.682
Where f prime equals zero, the tangent is
horizontal.

58
00:03:59.682 --> 00:04:06.161
A zero derivative identifies a critical point
candidate, not automatically a maximum or

59
00:04:06.161 --> 00:04:06.659
minimum.

60
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:13.021
For x squared, f prime changes from negative
to positive at zero, matching the parabola's

61
00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:13.445
minimum.

62
00:04:13.445 --> 00:04:18.228
But another function can have a horizontal
tangent and continue increasing.

63
00:04:18.228 --> 00:04:23.472
The derivative gives local evidence;
interpreting a larger pattern requires

64
00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:27.667
checking how that evidence changes around the
point.

65
00:04:27.794 --> 00:04:32.948
Not every graph has an ordinary finite tangent
slope everywhere.

66
00:04:32.948 --> 00:04:38.986
At a jump or hole, the function is not
continuous, so it cannot be differentiable

67
00:04:38.986 --> 00:04:39.389
there.

68
00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:44.580
At a sharp corner, the left-hand and
right-hand slopes approach different values.

69
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.840
At a cusp, the slopes can diverge with
incompatible signs.

70
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.834
At a vertical tangent, the slope becomes
unbounded rather than approaching a finite

71
00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:55.295
number.

72
00:04:55.295 --> 00:05:00.218
Differentiability is therefore a stronger
condition than continuity.

73
00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:04.618
If a function is differentiable at a point, it
is continuous there.

74
00:05:04.618 --> 00:05:09.485
The reverse is not guaranteed: the
absolute-value function is continuous at zero

75
00:05:09.485 --> 00:05:14.351
but has a corner, so its derivative at zero
does not exist.

76
00:05:14.498 --> 00:05:18.134
Suppose s of t gives position along a line.

77
00:05:18.134 --> 00:05:22.254
A secant slope over a time interval gives
average velocity.

78
00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:26.960
The derivative s prime of t gives
instantaneous velocity.

79
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:33.329
Its sign indicates direction relative to the
chosen coordinate axis, while its magnitude is

80
00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:33.784
speed.

81
00:05:33.784 --> 00:05:37.317
Differentiate velocity to obtain acceleration.

82
00:05:37.317 --> 00:05:42.685
Positive acceleration does not necessarily
mean the object is speeding up; speed

83
00:05:42.685 --> 00:05:48.949
increases when velocity and acceleration have
the same sign and decreases when their signs

84
00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:49.396
differ.

85
00:05:49.396 --> 00:05:51.641
Units also reveal meaning.

86
00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:58.525
If position is measured in meters and time in
seconds, velocity uses meters per second and

87
00:05:58.525 --> 00:06:01.106
acceleration uses meters per second squared.

88
00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.268
The derivative is also the coefficient of a
local linear model.

89
00:06:05.268 --> 00:06:11.340
At x equals a, the tangent line has value f of
a and slope f prime of a.

90
00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:18.189
Its equation is L of x equals f of a plus f
prime of a times x minus a.

91
00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:22.756
For x close to a, L of x can approximate f of
x.

92
00:06:22.756 --> 00:06:29.174
For example, to estimate the square root of
four point one, use f of x equals square root

93
00:06:29.174 --> 00:06:30.957
x at a equals four.

94
00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:37.049
The value is two and the derivative is one
fourth, so the estimate is two plus one fourth

95
00:06:37.049 --> 00:06:40.434
times zero point one, or two point zero two
five.

96
00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:46.314
The method works locally; accuracy generally
decreases farther from the base point.

97
00:06:46.442 --> 00:06:54.045
Let f of x equal three x squared minus two x,
and find the tangent at x equals one.

98
00:06:54.045 --> 00:06:59.504
The derivative function is six x minus two, so
f prime of one is four.

99
00:06:59.504 --> 00:07:05.090
The point on the original graph is one comma f
of one, which is one comma one.

100
00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:10.575
Using point-slope form, y minus one equals
four times x minus one.

101
00:07:10.575 --> 00:07:16.015
This example separates three objects that
students often mix together: f of one is the

102
00:07:16.015 --> 00:07:22.906
output one; f prime of one is the slope four;
and the tangent line is a new linear equation

103
00:07:22.906 --> 00:07:23.994
built from both.

104
00:07:23.994 --> 00:07:28.394
Always compute the point and the slope before
writing the tangent line.

105
00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:33.349
When solving a derivative problem from first
principles, use five checks.

106
00:07:33.349 --> 00:07:37.558
First, identify the two inputs and the
interval change h.

107
00:07:37.558 --> 00:07:43.170
Second, form the difference quotient with
parentheses around the entire numerator.

108
00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.550
Third, simplify while h is nonzero.

109
00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:52.022
Fourth, take the limit only after the
removable algebraic obstacle is handled.

110
00:07:52.022 --> 00:07:57.571
Fifth, interpret the result with a slope,
rate, units, or tangent line.

111
00:07:57.571 --> 00:08:03.540
Common errors include substituting zero too
early, dropping parentheses, confusing f of a

112
00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:09.050
with f prime of a, and assuming every
continuous point is differentiable.

113
00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:14.705
The algebra matters, but the interpretation is
what tells you whether the answer belongs to

114
00:08:14.705 --> 00:08:15.836
the original problem.

115
00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:20.006
The derivative begins with two points and an
average rate of change.

116
00:08:20.006 --> 00:08:23.998
A limit brings those points together without
dividing by zero.

117
00:08:23.998 --> 00:08:29.866
When the limit exists, it gives the tangent
slope and instantaneous rate at one input.

118
00:08:29.866 --> 00:08:34.457
Repeating the process across inputs creates
the derivative function.

119
00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:39.551
Its sign and value describe local behavior,
its failures mark important geometric

120
00:08:39.551 --> 00:08:45.495
features, its physical units describe rates
such as velocity, and its value supplies the

121
00:08:45.495 --> 00:08:48.042
slope of a local linear approximation.

122
00:08:48.042 --> 00:08:50.516
These are not unrelated definitions.

123
00:08:50.516 --> 00:08:53.692
They are different views of the same
mathematical object.

124
00:08:53.692 --> 00:08:59.341
If you can move fluently among the quotient,
the graph, the units, and the application, you

125
00:08:59.341 --> 00:09:03.930
understand the derivative at a foundation
strong enough for the rules and applications

126
00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:04.636
that follow.
